---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { CONTACT_FORM_LIVE, REACTIONS_LIVE, SITE, TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY, withBase } from '../lib/site';
---
<BaseLayout title="Privacy" description={`Privacy for ${SITE.title} (${SITE.domain}).`}>
  <p class="page-kicker">Legal</p>
  <h1 class="page-title">Privacy</h1>
  <div class="article">
    <p class="lede">
      {SITE.title} does not run accounts or a mailing list.{CONTACT_FORM_LIVE ? ' The about page can email the desk.' : ' The contact form on the about page opens soon.'} Last updated 26 September 2026.
    </p>
    <div class="article__body">
      <p>
        The pages do not ask you to make an account on {SITE.domain}. The RSS feed is a public file.
        It does not record who subscribes.
      </p>
      <p>
        Comments under a story are built into the page from the desk's own records. Nothing is
        loaded from a comment host when you read one. To post a comment, the form on the story page
        sends your name, the comment text, how long the form was open before you sent it, and a
        check from Cloudflare Turnstile that you are not a bot, to comments.{SITE.domain}. No email
        address is asked. A comment waits for the desk to read and approve it before anyone else
        sees it.
      </p>
      <p>
        The comments Worker is the desk's own program, not a vendor's. Nothing about a comment goes
        through a third-party comment host or an advertising tracker. This is what the desk keeps
        when you send a comment, and for how long. The desk deletes old data in a clean-up that runs
        once a night, so anything kept "for 30 days" is deleted at the first clean-up after those 30
        days: at most 31 days after it was stored. Deleted data can remain in the database's
        restorable backups for up to 30 days more, until those backups expire.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Your IP address, only as a salted, shortened hash. The address itself is never stored.
          The hash is kept for 30 days and deleted within a day after that. It is used to limit how
          many comments one visitor can send, to spot the same comment sent twice, to judge how far to trust a new comment, and to
          enforce a ban. When the desk bans a visitor, the hash and a short note of the reason are
          kept for 30 days from the ban and deleted within a day after that. While you send,
          Cloudflare's rate limit and its Turnstile check see the address itself; that handling is Cloudflare's.
        </li>
        <li>
          A trust score for the comment, worked out from its content and from that hash's recent
          history, and the number of links in it. These stay with the comment's record.
        </li>
        <li>
          How long the form was open before you sent it. This is kept for 30 days and deleted within a
          day after that.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your name and comment text, while the desk reviews the comment. If the desk rejects it,
          the name and text are kept for 30 days after the review and erased within a day after that.
        </li>
        <li>
          If the desk removes a comment, its name and text are erased from the desk's database at
          once. A bare record that a comment existed stays: its id, story, times, trust score and link
          count. The database keeps restorable backups for up to 30 days, so erased text can remain in
          those backups until they expire.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A published comment is public. It appears on the story's page, on the{' '}
        <a href={withBase('/campfire/')}>Campfire</a> page with the other newest comments, and in the
        story page's structured data (the machine-readable summary search engines read). The site's
        source is public on GitHub, and a published comment is part of its history there. Removing a
        comment takes it off the site and erases its name and text from the desk's database, but it
        cannot erase it from that public history.
      </p>
      <p>
        To ask for a comment's removal, use{' '}
        {CONTACT_FORM_LIVE ? (
          <>the <a href={withBase('/about/#contact')}>contact form</a></>
        ) : (
          'the contact form on the about page, which opens before comments do'
        )}, and quote the comment's id, shown next to its time.
      </p>
      {REACTIONS_LIVE && (
        <p>
          Reacting to a story (the "React" button) sends the story's address and your choice to
          comments.{SITE.domain}. The desk keeps your choice with your IP address as the same salted,
          shortened hash it keeps for comments, for 30 days from your last change: the hash makes one
          visitor count once per story and enforces a ban. If your choice could not reach the desk,
          the page sends it again the next time you open that story.
          Within a day after those 30 days, the nightly clean-up folds your choice into the story's
          anonymous totals and deletes the hash. Removing your reaction deletes it as soon as the removal
          reaches the desk; if it cannot reach the desk right away, the page sends it again the next time
          you open that story. A removed
          or folded reaction, hash included, can remain in the database's restorable backups for up
          to 30 days more. Because it
          is the same hash, for those 30 days the desk's database can tell that a comment and a
          reaction came from the same internet address (which several people on one network can
          share). The desk does not use that link for anything but bans, and never to follow what you
          read or react to. While you react,
          Cloudflare's rate limit sees the address itself; that handling is Cloudflare's. The totals
          are public and are baked into the story's page. Your browser remembers your own choice in
          its local storage (not a cookie) for 30 days, so the button can show it; any story page you
          open deletes remembered choices older than that, and clearing site data forgets them at
          once.
        </p>
      )}
      <p>
        {SITE.domain} is hosted on Cloudflare Pages. The host receives the ordinary details of a web request: the address of the page, the IP
        address, and the browser’s user agent. That handling is Cloudflare’s, under its own policy.
      </p>
      <p>
        On {SITE.domain} the site also loads Google Analytics. The tag sends Google the page address
        and stores a cookie so a later visit from the same browser can be counted together. The site
        does not sell this information.
      </p>
      <p>
        The type is loaded from Google Fonts. Your browser requests those font files from Google
        when it draws a page.
      </p>
      <p>
        Some stories include a YouTube video. Nothing is requested from YouTube until you press
        play. Then the player loads from YouTube’s privacy-enhanced domain (youtube-nocookie.com),
        and YouTube receives the ordinary details of that request under its own policy.
      </p>
      {CONTACT_FORM_LIVE ? (
        <p>
          The <a href={withBase('/about/#contact')}>contact form</a> asks for a name, an email address,
          and a note. Sending it goes to contact.{SITE.domain}, a small Cloudflare program that checks
          the note and asks Cloudflare Email to deliver it to the desk, with your address as the
          reply-to. Nothing is stored in a database. To stop floods, Cloudflare counts how many notes
          come from your IP address in the last minute; the count is not kept. A hidden field is there
          for spam; filling it drops the note.{TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY && ' The form also runs Cloudflare Turnstile, which checks your browser for signs of a bot before the note is accepted.'}
        </p>
      ) : (
        <p>
          The contact form on the about page is not open yet, so it collects nothing. When it opens,
          this page will say what it sends and where.
        </p>
      )}
      <p>
        The desk does not sell personal information, because the site does not collect an account
        or a subscriber list. The <a href={withBase('/terms/')}>terms of use</a> cover how the
        stories may be read and quoted.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
